\section{Introduction}\label{sec:interprocedural-intro}

The dataflow analyses of the preceding chapters operate on a single procedure at a time. This \emph{intraprocedural} approach treats each procedure call as a black box: the call may modify any global variable, and any parameter may take any value. While this is sound, it is imprecise---often wildly so. Consider a function \texttt{swap(x, y)} that exchanges its arguments. An intraprocedural analysis cannot determine that after the call, \texttt{x} holds the old value of \texttt{y} and vice versa. It must conservatively assume that both \texttt{x} and \texttt{y} could be anything.

\emph{Interprocedural analysis} extends dataflow analysis across procedure boundaries, enabling the analyzer to reason about the effects of calls, the flow of values through parameters and returns, and the calling context. The result is dramatically more precise information, at the cost of significantly greater complexity.

The fundamental challenge of interprocedural analysis is the \emph{context problem}: a procedure may be called from many different sites, and the information valid at one call site may not be valid at another. A naive approach that merges information from all call sites loses precision. Context-sensitive analyses address this by distinguishing different calling contexts.

\section{Call Graphs and Call Sites}\label{sec:call-graphs}

\subsection{The Call Graph}\label{sec:call-graph-def}

\begin{definition}[Call Graph]\label{def:call-graph}
A \textbf{call graph} is a directed graph $CG = (V, E)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $V$ is the set of procedures (functions, methods) in the program.
  \item $E \subseteq V \times V$ is the set of call edges: $(p, q) \in E$ if procedure $p$ contains a call site that may invoke procedure $q$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Call Site]\label{def:call-site}
A \textbf{call site} is a program point at which a procedure call occurs. A call site $c$ in procedure $p$ is associated with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The \textbf{actual parameters}: the arguments passed at the call.
  \item The \textbf{return site}: the program point to which control returns after the call.
  \item The set of \textbf{possible targets}: the procedures that might be invoked.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

For statically typed languages with monomorphic dispatch, the possible targets at a call site can often be determined exactly. For languages with dynamic dispatch (virtual methods, higher-order functions), the set of possible targets may be larger and must be approximated.

\begin{definition}[Supergraph]\label{def:supergraph}
The \textbf{interprocedural supergraph} (or system dependence graph) $G^* = (N^*, E^*)$ is constructed from the CFGs of all procedures by adding:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Call edges} from each call site node to the entry node of each possible target.
  \item \textbf{Return edges} from each exit node to the return site of each corresponding call.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

