\subsection{Invalidating Cached Summaries Through Caller Dependencies}

Cached interprocedural facts can remain valid after a local edit
if they did not use a changed body or a changed call target set.
To find the rest, maintain a reverse dependency index whose edges
point from a summary key to the keys that used it. A key may include
the calling context as well as the procedure identity. Record a
dependency even when a callee summary contributes no facts, because
an edit could introduce one. Start from every key with a changed
body or changed target assumptions, including old keys that no
longer have a body. Keep the previous dependency index intact
throughout this traversal.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Changed keys $C$, previous reverse dependency index $R$
\State $D\gets C$; $W\gets$ queue containing $C$
\While{$W$ is nonempty}
  \State remove $k$ from $W$
  \ForAll{recorded dependents $c$ in $R[k]$}
    \If{$c\notin D$}
      \State insert $c$ in $D$ \Comment{Mark before following cycles}
      \State enqueue $c$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\State evict all keys in $D$ \Comment{Do not seed with stale facts}
\State \Return $D$ for recomputation on the new graph
\end{sudocode}

Suppose \texttt{readFlag} calls \texttt{flag}, and
\texttt{main} calls \texttt{readFlag}. A separate
\texttt{format} summary depends on neither. Change
\texttt{flag} to return 2 instead of 1. Starting with its key,
the queue reaches \texttt{readFlag} and then \texttt{main}.
The cached \texttt{format} result survives. If a new dispatch
target is added at a call in \texttt{main}, its key must be
seeded even if the old index has no edge for the new target.

\Cref{fig:visual-65} uses the reverse of the calling direction:
an edit propagates from a changed summary to the callers that used it.

