After each queue removal, every visited dependent is either
already processed or scheduled for inspection. The cost is linear
in the reached keys and reverse edges, including across recursive
cycles. This procedure only determines what to evict. Recompute
mutually recursive summaries with a fixed-point solver using the
new equations. Keeping the old facts as the initial solution can
prevent a may-analysis from removing facts that an edit makes
impossible.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:interprocedural-summary}

Interprocedural analysis extends the power of dataflow analysis beyond procedure boundaries, enabling reasoning about the flow of values and definitions across calls. The key challenge is the context problem: distinguishing different calling contexts to avoid spurious information flow. The call-string approach achieves this by explicitly tracking call histories, while the functional approach uses procedure summaries. The IFDS framework provides an efficient algorithm for the important special case of distributive subset problems, and the IDE framework extends this to environment problems like constant propagation. Scalability remains an active area of research, particularly for languages with dynamic dispatch and large codebases. Demand-driven and incremental approaches offer practical solutions for specific use cases.

\section{The Call-String Approach in Detail}\label{sec:call-string-detail}

The \emph{call-string} approach, introduced by Sharir and Pnueli~\cite{sharir1981framework}, is one of the most intuitive methods for achieving context sensitivity. The core idea is to tag each dataflow fact with the sequence of call sites that led to its computation.

\begin{definition}[Call String]\label{def:call-string}
A \textbf{call string} is a sequence of call sites $[s_1, s_2, \ldots, s_k]$ representing the path of calls from the program entry to the current procedure. The \emph{length} of the call string is $k$, the number of call sites in the sequence.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Call-String Analysis]\label{def:call-string-analysis}
In a \textbf{$k$-call-string} analysis, each dataflow fact at a program point $p$ in procedure $f$ is associated with the last $k$ call sites on the path from the entry to $f$. Two facts with different call strings are kept separate, even if they occur at the same program point.
\end{definition}

\subsection{1-CFA vs.\ 0-CFA}\label{sec:k-cfa}

The parameter $k$ controls the precision/cost tradeoff:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{0-CFA} ($k = 0$): No call-string information. All calling contexts are merged. This is equivalent to context-insensitive analysis.
  \item \textbf{1-CFA} ($k = 1$): Each fact is tagged with the most recent call site. This distinguishes direct callers but merges recursive calls.
  \item \textbf{$k$-CFA} ($k > 1$): Each fact is tagged with the last $k$ call sites. Higher $k$ gives more precision but increases the analysis cost by a factor of $O(|S|^k)$, where $|S|$ is the number of call sites.
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[Call-String Complexity]\label{thm:call-string-complexity}
A $k$-CFA analysis has time complexity $O(|E| \cdot |D| \cdot |S|^k)$, where $|E|$ is the number of edges in the supergraph, $|D|$ is the domain size, and $|S|$ is the number of call sites. For $k \geq 1$, this is exponential in $k$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Call-String Cloning}\label{sec:call-string-cloning}

A practical implementation technique for call-string analysis is \emph{procedure cloning}: creating separate copies of a procedure for each distinct calling context.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Procedure Cloning for Call-String Analysis}
\label{alg:proc-cloning}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Program $P$ with call graph $G$, context-sensitivity depth $k$
\Ensure Cloned program $P'$ with context-sensitive analysis
\State Compute call strings of length $k$ for each procedure
\ForAll{procedures $f$ with call strings $cs_1, cs_2, \ldots, cs_m$}
  \State Create copies $f_1, f_2, \ldots, f_m$ of $f$
  \For{each call site $s$ calling $f$}
    \State Determine the call string $cs_i$ at $s$
    \State Redirect $s$ to call $f_i$
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\State Run context-insensitive analysis on $P'$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{example}[Procedure Cloning]\label{ex:proc-cloning}
Consider:
\begin{verbatim}
  void main() {
    int x = 5;
    f(x);
    int y = 10;
    f(y);
  }
  void f(int a) {
    g(a);
  }
  void g(int b) {
    // What is b?
  }
\end{verbatim}
With 1-CFA, $g$ is cloned into $g_1$ (called from $f$ when $f$ is called with $x$) and $g_2$ (called from $f$ when $f$ is called with $y$). In $g_1$, $b = 5$; in $g_2$, $b = 10$. Without cloning, $b \in \{5, 10\}$ at the analysis point.
\end{example}

\section{Summary-Based (Functional) Approach}\label{sec:summary-based-detail}

The \emph{summary-based} or \emph{functional} approach represents the effect of a procedure as a \emph{function} from input dataflow values to output dataflow values. This avoids the exponential blowup of the call-string approach.

\begin{definition}[Procedure Summary Function]\label{def:summary-fn}
The \textbf{summary function} for a procedure $f$ is a function $\phi_f : D^{in} \to D^{out}$ that maps the dataflow values at $f$'s entry to the dataflow values at $f$'s exit. For a distributive flow function $f_{path}$ along a path through $f$, the summary is $\phi_f(d) = \bigsqcup_{path} f_{path}(d)$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Computing Summaries Iteratively}\label{sec:summary-iterative}

Summaries are computed by a fixedpoint iteration over the call graph:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Summary-Based Interprocedural Analysis}
\label{alg:summary-ipa}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Call graph $G$, initial summaries $\phi_f^0 = \lambda d.\top$ for all $f$
\Ensure Converged summaries $\phi_f^*$ for all $f$
\Repeat
  \ForAll{procedures $f$ in bottom-up order}
    \State Compute $\phi_f^{i+1}$ using current summaries of callees
    \State $\Delta = \phi_f^{i+1} \sqcap \phi_f^i$
    \If{$\Delta \neq \bot$}
      \State Mark callers of $f$ as changed
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\Until{no summaries change}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[Summary Convergence]\label{thm:summary-convergence}
For a finite-height lattice and monotone summary functions, the summary-based iteration converges in at most $O(|F| \cdot h)$ iterations, where $|F|$ is the number of procedures and $h$ is the lattice height.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Summary Representation}\label{sec:summary-representation}

For distributive functions over a finite lattice, the summary function can be represented compactly:

\begin{definition}[Linear Summary Representation]\label{def:linear-summary}
A distributive function $f : 2^S \to 2^S$ over a powerset lattice can be represented by its values on singleton sets: $f(X) = \bigcup_{x \in X} f(\{x\})$. This requires storing $|S|$ values, one for each singleton.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Summary Representation]\label{ex:summary-rep}
For reaching definitions with definition set $D = \{d_1, d_2, d_3\}$:
\begin{verbatim}
  Summary of f:
    f({d1}) = {d1, d3}    // d1 survives, d3 is generated
    f({d2}) = {}           // d2 is killed
    f({d3}) = {d3}         // d3 survives
  Therefore:
    f({d1, d2}) = {d1, d3}
    f({d1, d2, d3}) = {d1, d3}
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\section{Handling Recursion in Interprocedural Analysis}\label{sec:recursion-handling}

Recursion poses a fundamental challenge for interprocedural analysis because the call graph contains cycles.

\subsection{Recursion-Summarization}\label{sec:recursion-summarization}

\begin{definition}[Recursive Summary]\label{def:recursive-summary}
For a recursive procedure $f$, the summary $\phi_f$ is defined as the \emph{least fixedpoint} of the equation:
\[
\phi_f = \lambda d.\; \bigsqcup_{path \in f} f_{path}(d, \phi_f)
\]
where $f_{path}$ may invoke $\phi_f$ recursively. This fixedpoint exists when the lattice has finite height and the functions are monotone.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Unrolling-Based Approach}\label{sec:unrolling-approach}

An alternative to recursive summarization is to \emph{unroll} the recursion to a bounded depth $d$:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Bounded Recursion Unrolling}
\label{alg:recursion-unroll}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Recursive procedure $f$, unrolling depth $d$
\Ensure Approximate summary $\phi_f^d$
\State Create $d$ copies of $f$: $f^1, f^2, \ldots, f^d$
\State In $f^i$, replace recursive calls with calls to $f^{i+1}$
\State In $f^d$, replace recursive calls with $\lambda d.\top$ (no information)
\State Compute summaries bottom-up: $\phi_{f^d}, \phi_{f^{d-1}}, \ldots, \phi_{f^1}$
\State Return $\phi_f^d = \phi_{f^1}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[Unrolling Soundness]\label{thm:unrolling-sound}
The summary computed by unrolling to depth $d$ is a sound over-approximation of the true recursive summary: $\phi_f^d \sqsupseteq \phi_f^*$. As $d \to \infty$, $\phi_f^d$ converges to $\phi_f^*$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Interprocedural Analysis for Object-Oriented Programs}\label{sec:ipa-oo}

Object-oriented languages introduce additional challenges for interprocedural analysis.

\subsection{Virtual Dispatch Resolution}\label{sec:virtual-dispatch}

\begin{definition}[Class Hierarchy Analysis]\label{def:cha}
\textbf{Class hierarchy analysis} (CHA) resolves virtual calls by examining the class hierarchy: if a method $m$ is declared in class $C$ and no subclass of $C$ overrides $m$, then the call target is uniquely determined.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rapid Type Analysis]\label{def:rta}
\textbf{Rapid type analysis} (RTA) iteratively refines the set of instantiated classes and the set of possible call targets. A method is a potential call target only if its declaring class (or a subclass that overrides it) is actually instantiated.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Rapid Type Analysis}
\label{alg:rta}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Program $P$, entry point $e$
\Ensure Call graph $G$ and instantiated class set $I$
\State $I \gets \emptyset$; $G \gets \{(e, \text{targets}(e))\}$
\State $W \gets \{e\}$
\While{$W \neq \emptyset$}
  \State Remove $m$ from $W$
  \ForAll{instructions $s$ in $m$}
    \If{$s$ is a \texttt{new} $C$()}
      \State $I \gets I \cup \{C\}$
      \State Add new call targets enabled by $C$ to $W$
    \ElsIf{$s$ is a virtual call $r.m'()$}
      \State Compute targets using $I$
      \State Add new edges to $G$ and targets to $W$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Devirtualization}\label{sec:devirtualization}

\begin{definition}[Devirtualization]\label{def:devirtualization}
\textbf{Devirtualization} replaces a virtual call with a direct call when the analysis determines that only one target is possible. This enables inlining and other interprocedural optimizations.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Devirtualization]\label{ex:devirtualization}
\begin{verbatim}
  // Before devirtualization:
  Animal a = new Dog();
  a.speak();  // virtual call
  
  // After CHA determines only Dog.speak() is possible:
  Animal a = new Dog();
  Dog.speak(a);  // direct call, can be inlined
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\section{Compositional vs.\ Non-Compositional Analysis}\label{sec:compositional-ipa}

\begin{definition}[Compositional Analysis]\label{def:compositional}
An interprocedural analysis is \textbf{compositional} if the analysis result for a procedure depends only on the procedure's code and the analysis results of its \emph{immediate} callees (via their summaries). The whole-program result is obtained by composing the per-procedure results.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Non-Compositional Analysis]\label{def:non-compositional}
A \textbf{non-compositional} analysis inlines all procedures (conceptually or literally) and analyzes the resulting monolithic program. This avoids the need for summaries but can lead to code explosion.
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Compositional} & \textbf{Non-Compositional} \\
\hline
Scalability & Good (summarize once) & Poor (code explosion) \\
Precision (recursive) & May lose precision & Exact (up to unrolling) \\
Incremental updates & Easy (re-summarize) & Hard (re-analyze all) \\
Implementation complexity & Higher & Lower \\
Memory usage & Lower & Higher \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Compositional vs.\ non-compositional interprocedural analysis.}
\label{tab:compositional-comparison}
\end{table}
